# Each of these is its own standalone project with its own git repo and
# Netlify site — nested inside this working tree for convenience, but never
# meant to be tracked/committed here. Ignored so none of them can get swept
# into a Portfolio commit (e.g. via VS Code's "Smart Commit" auto-stage,
# which already did this twice and broke Netlify's build via a bogus
# submodule reference with no matching .gitmodules entry).
